   "id": "h2-40",
   "name": "Heat transfer: conduction"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Heat moves from hot places to cooler places in three ways: conduction, convection and radiation.\nConduction is the transfer of heat through a solid, from particle to particle, without the solid moving. The hot particles vibrate faster and pass the energy to their neighbours.\nGood conductors let heat pass easily: metals (copper, aluminium, iron, steel). Poor conductors (insulators) do not: wood, plastic, cloth, air, clay.\nExamples in the kitchen: heat passing through the base of a pot from the fire to the food; frying, where the pan conducts heat to the food; a metal spoon in hot soup getting hot.\nAdvantages: metal pots heat food quickly and evenly.\nDisadvantages: metal handles get hot and burn the hands; heat is lost through thin walls. That is why pot handles are made of wood or plastic, and oven gloves are used.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "x": "a metal spoon left in a pot of hot soup gets hot, but a wooden spoon does not"
  },
  "problem": "Explain why {x}.",
  "steps": [
   "Metal is a good conductor: heat passes along it quickly by conduction.",
   "Wood is a poor conductor (an insulator), so little heat reaches the handle."
  ],
  "answer": "Metal conducts heat well; wood does not.",
  "checks": []
 },
